    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A 5000 watt generator runs the refrigerator, a chest freezer, a gas furnace blower, a sump pump, the lights and the internet at the same time, with room for one more motor to start. In summer it swaps the furnace for a window air conditioner. With a 240V twist-lock outlet it can start a 1 HP well pump if you clear the deck first. It will not run central air, an electric range, an electric water heater or a dryer in any useful way. The rest of this page is the arithmetic behind those sentences, so you can redo it with your own appliance labels.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="the-rule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Two-Number Rule for Any 5000 Watt Load List
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every load list on this page is checked against two numbers, and you need both. The first is the running total: the sum of the running watts of everything that is on at the same time. The second is the worst-case start: the running total of everything else, plus the starting watts of the biggest motor that might kick on while the rest are running. If the running total fits under the generator's running rating and the worst-case start fits under its starting rating, the list works. If either one fails, the breaker trips or the motor stalls.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is that "5000 watt" is not one rating. Generators sold in this class are rated anywhere from about 3,900 to 5,500 running watts, with starting or peak figures from 5,000 to just under 7,000. A unit named for its peak watts may only carry 4,000 continuously. Before you add up anything, find the running watts on the spec sheet, on the fuel you will actually use, because propane typically knocks another 10 percent or so off. Our <a href="/best-5000-watt-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">5000 watt generator picks</a> list both numbers for every model.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then leave margin. Generator makers and electricians commonly suggest keeping the continuous load to around 80 percent of the running rating. On a unit rated 5,000 running watts, that is a 4,000 watt working budget. The margin covers the things a spreadsheet misses: an old refrigerator drawing more than its label, a furnace blower on a dirty filter, voltage sag on a long cord, and the fact that an engine on a hot afternoon makes less power than it did on the test stand.
          </p>

          <p>
            All the appliance figures below are typical nameplate ranges, the same ones used in our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">generator wattage chart</a>. They are not measurements of your appliances. Read the labels on your own refrigerator, furnace and pumps, or put a plug-in watt meter on the cord-connected ones for a day, and swap your numbers in. The method matters more than our figures, because two refrigerators of the same size can differ by several hundred watts.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3,900-5,500W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Running range sold as 5000W</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~4,000W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">80% working budget on a 5kW unit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~2,500W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 120V leg in 240V mode</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">7,200W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">L14-30 cord ceiling</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="winter-list" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Load List One: A Winter Outage With Gas Heat
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the list most buyers have in mind: keep the food cold, keep the house warm, keep the basement dry and keep the lights and Wi-Fi on. It assumes a gas or propane furnace, where the generator only powers the blower motor, igniter and control board, not the heat itself. An oil burner adds its burner motor but lands in a similar range.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Load</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Running watts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Starting watts</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Refrigerator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">700</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chest freezer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas furnace blower and controls</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sump pump, 1/2 HP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,600</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">LED lights, router, TV, chargers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Running total, all on</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">n/a</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Worst case: sump starts while the rest run</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">n/a</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,400 + 2,600 = 5,000</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The running total is 3,400 watts, which sits inside the 4,000 watt working budget of a unit rated 5,000 running watts with 600 to spare. The worst-case start is the sump pump kicking on while the other four loads are already running: 2,400 watts of running load plus a 2,600 watt start, or 5,000 watts for about a second. A generator rated around 6,000 starting watts handles that comfortably. One rated at 5,000 peak watts, which is common among inverter models in this class, is right on the line.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case that actually breaks this list is two motors starting together. That happens most often right after the generator is started or reset, when everything is plugged in and every thermostat and float switch is calling at once. The refrigerator and sump pump starting in the same second, with the furnace and freezer already running, is 2,200 plus 2,600 plus 1,700, or 6,500 watts. Very few generators in this class will carry that. It is a sequencing problem, not a size problem, and section six covers how to avoid it.
          </p>

          <p>
            What fits in the 600 watts left over? A second refrigerator in the garage, a CPAP machine, a laptop and a modest television all fit. A microwave or coffee maker at 1,000 to 1,500 watts fits only if you switch something off for the few minutes it runs, which in practice means the freezer, since a full chest freezer holds temperature for many hours with the lid shut. A 1,500 watt space heater does not fit alongside this list at all; on a 5kW unit it is the furnace or the space heater, not both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice that two of these five loads, the furnace and the sump pump, are usually hardwired or on dedicated circuits. Extension cords will not reach them. To run this list as written you need a 240V outlet on the generator and a transfer switch or interlock at the panel; our walkthrough on <a href="/can-a-generator-power-a-furnace-refrigerator-and-sump-pump/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">powering a furnace, refrigerator and sump pump</a> covers that hookup in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="summer-list" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Load List Two: A Summer Outage With a Window AC
          </h2>

          <p>
            Summer swaps the furnace for cooling, and cooling is where a 5000 watt generator starts to feel its size. A 10,000 BTU window air conditioner typically runs at 1,000 to 1,200 watts and surges to 2,200 to 3,000 when its compressor starts. That is the largest start on the list, bigger than the sump pump, and it repeats every time the compressor cycles on, not just once.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Load</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Running watts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Starting watts</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Refrigerator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">700</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chest freezer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Window AC, 10,000 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sump pump, 1/2 HP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,600</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lights, router, box fans, chargers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Running total, all on</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,700</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">n/a</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Worst case: AC starts while the rest run</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">n/a</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,600 + 3,000 = 5,600</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The running total climbs to 3,700 watts, still inside the 4,000 watt budget but with only 300 watts of slack. The worst-case start is now the air conditioner: 2,600 watts already running plus a 3,000 watt compressor start, or 5,600 watts. A generator rated 6,000 to 7,000 starting watts carries it. A 5,000 peak watt inverter model probably does not, and the symptom is the AC humming, failing to start and the generator's overload light coming on a second later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hot weather makes all of this worse. Air conditioner compressors draw more when the outdoor coil is hot, refrigerators run longer duty cycles, and the generator's engine makes less power in high heat. On a 95 degree afternoon, plan on the summer list behaving like a 4,000 running watt list rather than a 3,700 one. If your house only has a sump pump because of spring thaw, unplugging it in a dry July buys back 1,000 watts and solves the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Can you run two window units? Usually, if one is small. A 5,000 to 6,000 BTU bedroom unit typically draws a few hundred watts running, so a large living room unit plus a small bedroom unit fits if the sump pump is off or the freezer is rotated. Two 10,000 BTU units plus the refrigerator and freezer runs past 3,800 watts before lights, which leaves no room for a start. In that case, cool one room well rather than two rooms badly.
          </p>

          <p>
            What you get on a 5kW unit in summer is one cool room, not a cool house. If whole-house cooling is the reason you are buying a generator, read our guide to <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-central-air-conditioning/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">sizing a generator for central air</a> before you buy anything; the answer is rarely a portable 5,000 watt unit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="well-pump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Load List Three: A 240V Well Pump on the L14-30
          </h2>

          <p>
            Houses on a private well are the hardest case for a 5000 watt generator, because no water means no flushing, no washing and no drinking water from the tap. Most residential well pumps run on 240 volts. That means the generator needs a 120/240V outlet, almost always a four-prong L14-30R twist-lock, and the pump has to be fed through a transfer switch or interlocked panel. A generator with only 120V outlets, or with a TT-30R or L5-30R as its only 30-amp outlet, cannot run a 240V pump at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            A typical 1 HP submersible pump runs at 1,000 to 1,200 watts and starts at 3,000 to 4,000. Use the high end, because well pumps start against the full weight of the water column and the pressure in the tank, and older pumps draw more than their nameplate. Now add the pump to the winter list. With the refrigerator, freezer, furnace and lights running at 2,400 watts and the pump starting at 4,000, the generator briefly has to supply 6,400 watts. That is past the starting rating of most units in this class.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now clear the deck. Switch off the freezer and the sump pump while the pump refills the pressure tank, which takes a minute or two, and the running load drops to 1,900 watts. Add the 4,000 watt pump start and the worst case is 5,900, which a generator rated 6,500 to 7,000 starting watts carries. Once the pump is running at 1,200 watts, the total is 3,100, and the freezer can go back on after the tank fills. That is the realistic way a 5000 watt generator runs a house with a well: the pump gets its turn, and the other motors wait.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a second constraint that catches people out. In 240V mode, a 120/240V generator splits its output across two 120V legs, so a 5,000 watt unit gives each leg roughly 2,500 watts. A 240V pump draws equally from both legs, but your 120V circuits are each on one leg. If the refrigerator, freezer and furnace all happen to be on leg A in your panel, leg A trips while leg B sits half empty. When the electrician wires your transfer switch, ask them to balance the essential circuits across both legs, and put the biggest 120V motors on opposite sides.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pump size decides whether any of this is possible. A 1/2 HP or 3/4 HP pump is comfortable. A 1 HP pump works with the deck cleared as above. A 1.5 HP or 2 HP pump typically starts past 5,000 watts by itself and needs a bigger machine; a <a href="/best-6500-watt-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">6,500 watt generator</a> or larger is the honest answer. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-well-pump/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what size generator runs a well pump</a> lists starting figures by horsepower, and a soft starter fitted to the pump control box can cut the start enough to make a borderline pump work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-wont-run" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What a 5000 Watt Generator Won't Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some loads are not a juggling problem. They are simply bigger than the generator, or so big that running them leaves nothing for anything else. The short list: central air conditioning, an electric range or oven, an electric tank water heater, an electric clothes dryer, an electric furnace or heat pump auxiliary heat strips, and a Level 2 EV charger at full rate. If your outage plan depends on any of these, a 5000 watt portable is the wrong tool, and no transfer switch or cord changes that.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Load</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical draw</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">On a 5kW generator</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Central AC, 3 ton</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,000-3,800 running, 9,000-12,000 starting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No; the start alone is double the generator</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electric water heater</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4,000-4,500 continuous</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Technically alone on the biggest units; nothing else</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electric dryer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5,000-5,500 running</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electric range, full use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Several elements plus oven, far above 5,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No; one element at a time at most</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electric furnace or heat strips</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Typically 5,000-20,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Space heater</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500 continuous</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, but it eats 30-40% of the budget</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Central air is the one people most want and the one that fails hardest. A 3 ton system runs at 3,000 to 3,800 watts, which on its own nearly fills a 5,000 watt generator, and its compressor starts at 9,000 to 12,000. A soft starter cuts that start sharply, and on a small system paired with the largest units in this class it can occasionally bring the running side within reach, but you would be running the air conditioner and almost nothing else. That is not an outage plan. A window unit in one room is.
          </p>

          <p>
            The electric water heater is the trap in the other direction, because the numbers look like they fit. A 4,500 watt element at 240V draws about 19 amps, which an L14-30 cord carries, and a generator rated 5,500 running watts can technically power it. But it is a continuous resistive load that runs for an hour or more to reheat a tank, which means the refrigerator, furnace and sump pump are off the whole time. A full tank stays usefully hot for a day or so; let it coast. Our page on <a href="/can-a-portable-generator-power-an-electric-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">running an electric water heater on a generator</a> goes through the edge cases.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kitchens are the last trap. A single range element can draw 1,500 to 2,500 watts and the oven more, all on a 240V circuit. On a 5kW generator, cooking means a microwave, a single induction or hot plate burner on a 120V outlet with other loads switched off, or the gas grill outside. A gas range usually only needs 120V for its igniter and clock, so it is a happy exception.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HONEST LINE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If the plan includes central air, an electric range or an electric dryer, buy for that plan, not this one.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 5000 watt generator covers essentials plus one comfort load. Running a house as if the power never went out typically takes 10,000 watts or more, which is the territory of our guide to what a 10,000 watt generator can run, or a standby unit.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="staging-starts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Staging Motor Starts So the Breaker Stays In
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every load list above works on paper only if motors start one at a time. On a normal day they mostly do, because compressors, blowers and pumps cycle on their own schedules. The danger moments are predictable: the first minute after you start the generator, the first minute after a breaker trip, and the moment the transfer switch flips from line to generator. At each of those moments every motor that wants to run tries to start at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix costs nothing. Start the generator with every load switched off, or with the transfer switch circuits all set to off, and let the engine warm up for a minute or two. Then add loads in order of size, biggest motor first: the well pump if you have one, then the sump pump or air conditioner, then the furnace, then the refrigerator and freezer, and the lights and electronics last. Wait until each motor is up to speed and the engine note has settled before adding the next, which usually takes 10 to 30 seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Biggest first matters because the biggest start is the one most likely to fail, and it fails least when nothing else is running. If you start the refrigerator, freezer and furnace first and then try the well pump, you have built the worst-case start yourself. If you start the pump first, the other motors start into a generator that has headroom for their smaller surges.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electronics go last for a different reason. Motor starts pull voltage down for a fraction of a second, and an open-frame generator's voltage regulator then overshoots slightly as it recovers. Furnace control boards, televisions and computers tolerate that better when they are not plugged in during the busiest moments. Most modern 5000 watt units have automatic voltage regulation and are fine with electronics once the load is steady, but there is no reason to expose them to the startup scramble. If you want the full background on waveform quality, see <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">inverter vs regular generators</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Listen to the generator. A healthy start makes the engine dip and recover in under a second. If the engine bogs for several seconds, the lights dim noticeably, or a motor hums without spinning, switch that motor off immediately. A stalled motor draws its full starting current continuously, which overheats the motor windings and is exactly what trips the generator's breaker. Take something else off, then try it again. When you have found an order that works for your house, write it on a card and tape it to the transfer switch, because nobody remembers it in the dark.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cord-and-inlet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The 30A Cord and Inlet: Where the Wiring Limits You
          </h2>

          <p>
            At 5,000 watts, the cord between the generator and the house is rarely the bottleneck, but it does set some rules. A standard L14-30 generator cord is a four-wire, 30 amp, 120/240V cord. At 240V it can carry 30 amps, or 7,200 watts, which is more than any 5000 watt generator makes. Each leg can carry 30 amps at 120V, or 3,600 watts, which is also more than the roughly 2,500 watts per leg the generator provides. So the generator's own breaker trips long before the cord is overloaded, as long as the cord is genuinely rated for 30 amps.
          </p>

          <p>
            That rating is where people cut corners. A 30 amp cord should be 10 gauge copper. A 12 gauge cord with L14-30 ends on it is a fire risk at full load, and a very long cord adds voltage drop that makes motors draw more current to start. For runs up to about 50 feet, 10 gauge is fine; beyond that, check the voltage drop. Our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">generator cord sizing guide</a> has the gauge-by-length table. Keep the cord short enough that the generator can still sit at least 20 feet from the house, doors and windows, as the CDC and generator manufacturers advise.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the house end, the cord plugs into a power inlet box, usually an L14-30 inlet mounted on an outside wall, which is wired to a manual transfer switch or a breaker interlock on your main panel. That is the only legal and safe way to feed household circuits; back-feeding through a dryer outlet with a double-male cord can energise the utility lines and kill a line worker. Our guides to the <a href="/best-generator-inlet-box/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best generator inlet boxes</a> and <a href="/how-to-hook-up-a-generator-to-your-house/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to hook up a generator to your house</a> walk through the parts and the installation.
          </p>

          <p>
            The generator's outlet decides what you can connect. An L14-30R gives you both legs and 240V; it is the outlet a 5000 watt home backup generator should have. A TT-30R is the 120V RV outlet and does not connect to a home transfer switch. An L5-30R is a 120V-only twist-lock with three prongs, and fed into a transfer switch it can only power one leg of your panel, which rules out the well pump and halves the circuits you can use. Some installers jumper both legs together for 120V-only generators, which can overload a shared neutral on multi-wire circuits; it is not a fix to do yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical consequence of all this: if you plan to grow into a larger generator later, buy the inlet and transfer switch for it now. A 50 amp inlet and a switch rated for a 50 amp generator cost a little more and save redoing the wiring. If you are confident 5,000 watts is enough, a 30 amp setup matched to the L14-30 is the standard, well-supported choice, and the <a href="/best-240v-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">240V generator guide</a> covers the outlet types in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning Around the Peak Watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The biggest number on the box is usually the starting or peak rating, good for a second or two. Your load list has to fit under the running watts on the fuel you actually use. A generator sold as 5,000 watts that runs at 3,900 on gasoline and 3,500 on propane is a very different machine from one rated 5,500 running.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging Everything In Before Starting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Starting the generator with every load connected means every motor tries to start at once, which is the single most common reason a correctly sized 5kW unit trips. Start it empty, then add loads one by one, biggest motor first, waiting for each to settle before the next.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Leg Balance on 240V</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                In 240V mode a 5,000 watt generator gives each leg about 2,500 watts. If your refrigerator, freezer and furnace all sit on one leg of the transfer switch, that leg trips while the other carries almost nothing. Ask the electrician to split the big 120V motors across both legs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 120V-Only Unit for a House With a Well</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A TT-30R or L5-30R outlet cannot run a 240V well pump or feed both legs of the panel. If you have a well or want the furnace and sump pump on a transfer switch, the generator needs a four-prong L14-30R. Check the panel photo before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the Water Heater as Just Another Load</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An electric water heater fits on paper on the biggest 5kW units, but it runs for an hour or more at 4,000 to 4,500 watts and shuts out everything else. A full tank stays hot for a day. Switch its breaker off for the outage and spend the watts on the food and the heat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 5000 watt generator run a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It will run the essentials of most houses with gas heat: refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump, lights and internet, all at once, with room for one motor to start. It will not run a house as if the power never went out, because central air, electric cooking, an electric water heater and a dryer are each too big. Think of it as an essentials-plus-one-comfort machine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 5000 watt generator run a refrigerator and a freezer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Easily. A typical refrigerator runs at about 700 watts and a chest freezer at about 500, so both together use roughly a quarter of a 5kW unit's running capacity. Their compressor starts, around 2,200 and 1,500 watts, are well within the starting rating. The only caution is not letting both start in the same second right after the generator comes on.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 5000 watt generator run a window air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, one 10,000 BTU unit alongside the refrigerator, freezer and lights. It typically runs at 1,000 to 1,200 watts and starts at up to about 3,000. With the rest of the list running, the worst-case start lands around 5,600 watts, so a model rated 6,000 or more starting watts handles it. A 5,000 peak watt inverter may struggle in hot weather.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 5000 watt generator run a well pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 1/2 to 1 HP well pump, yes, if the generator has a 120/240V L14-30R outlet and you connect through a transfer switch. Clear other motors off while the pump starts, because a 1 HP pump can surge to 4,000 watts. A 1.5 HP or larger pump usually needs a bigger generator or a soft starter on the pump control box.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 5000 watt generator run central air?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in any practical sense. A 3 ton system runs at 3,000 to 3,800 watts and starts at 9,000 to 12,000, so the start alone is about twice what the generator can deliver. A soft starter cuts the surge, but the running draw would still leave almost nothing for the refrigerator and furnace. One window unit in a bedroom is the realistic summer plan at this size.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 5000 watt generator run on a tank of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on load more than size. Many 5kW open-frame models are rated for somewhere around 8 to 13 hours at a quarter to half load on a full tank of four to six gallons, and less at the 3,400 to 3,700 watt loads in this guide. Check the spec sheet's runtime figure and the load it was quoted at, and plan to refuel at least twice a day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 5000 watts enough, or should I buy bigger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is enough if your list is the essentials plus one comfort load, you have gas heat, and any well pump is 1 HP or smaller. Move up to the 6,500 to 8,000 watt class if you have a larger well pump, want two air conditioners, or do not want to stage loads. Move up to 10,000 watts or a standby unit if central air or electric cooking is non-negotiable.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

