        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A 5000 watt generator is the size where a house with gas heat stops being uncomfortable in an outage. It carries the refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump and lights together, swaps in a window air conditioner in summer, and starts a 1 HP well pump if you give the pump its turn. The arithmetic is simple: running total under about 80 percent of the running rating, and the biggest start plus everything else under the starting rating.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            What it will not do is run central air, electric cooking, an electric water heater or a dryer, and staging loads does not change that. If those are on your list, size up. If they are not, buy a unit with real running watts on your fuel and an L14-30R outlet, and our <a href="/best-5000-watt-generator/" class="text-[#38BDF8] font-semibold hover:underline">5000 watt generator picks</a> are the place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

